  the two concerns can change independently. Cost: a future login path must remember to store. There's one call site
  today, and the fix if that ever bites is approach B. Complexity: low.

  [1mB. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m writes to the session store itself.[0m
  [38;5;153mauth.js[39m imports [38;5;153msession.js[39m and stores the identity as a side effect of a successful login. Callers can't forget. Cost:
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m acquires a hidden side effect, testing it now requires a storage stub, and auth is permanently coupled to
  storage — a bad trade for saving one line at one call site. Complexity: low.

  [1mC. Observable session store.[0m
  As A, plus a subscriber list so forms re-render when identity changes. Buys reactivity for the forms you mentioned.
  But nothing today needs to [3mreact[0m to a login — the forms will read the value when they submit. This is an event system
  bought for a requirement that hasn't arrived. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It's the only one that keeps the new module boundary clean in both directions, and it's the cheapest to
  upgrade to B or C later if the need actually shows up. C is YAGNI today.

  One scope note that comes with any of them: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. I'd keep it a stub and
  have it return a fake [38;5;153muserId[39m, so this change stays about the identity plumbing. Making the network call real is a
  separate piece of work.
